re rust M5.2

The proposal reshapes shared rows that the Go lanes use:

• the shared output digest changes again, so Go summaries rebuild again and the M2/M3 lanes must pick that up;

• the shared import rows are rebuilt as one row per route;

• five new "language-neutral" tables are invented, which only Rust fills today.

That cross-lane coupling is the problem.

The answer changes from d76d8566f8 (#6337, the #6141 lexical-replay fix) are only partly intended, so I haven't approved a refresh of the frozen gate expectations yet.

- **Intended:** `ParameterizedTypeName.kt:103:5` losing its TypeName hierarchy diagnostic, with the same status and constructor target. It was in the attribution I accepted.
- **Plausibly intended:** the six work deltas, with the scope pins unchanged. Fewer retained hierarchy branches would shift work this way, but I've asked for the per-category numbers before accepting them.
- **Not explained:** the native Scala control at `demo/Greet.scala:8:14` now differs. The #6141 session reported that no control changed, so either their replay missed it or something else caused it. I've asked for its payloads before and after.

Once every difference is classified, I'll approve one refresh of all four expectation sets at a named master SHA:
- the 130 payloads;
- the nine answers;
- the 50 work records;
- the J4 answer file.

Until then, M4-4c compares against a fresh baseline built from its exact merge parents, which is correct.

I confirmed that Rust `ADAPTER_VERSION` v45 is free for #6365. Master is at v44, and no open PR changes the Rust adapter version or the Rust salt. The requesting session has accepted all four conditions:
- resolve any cache-identity conflict by hand at their pre-PR master merge;
- run the resolution-facts golden and the Rust shared-closer goldens, attributing every changed line;
- run the issue_5133 early-cutoff tests;
- list the 4 rotated golden files and the 70 IDs in the PR body.

I also told the Rust SAST session to expect Rust semantic IDs to rotate when #6365 lands.

Still waiting on the peer's diff for the Scala control at `demo/Greet.scala:8:14`, and on the per-category work deltas. Those decide whether I approve refreshing the frozen JVM gate expectations.

The peer accepted all five decisions, with one correction: #6335 has already landed, in batch #6372. If the isolated-binary check does find a Python change leaking into Scala, the fix therefore goes to master. Their combined baseline already contains #6335's code and matches the frozen Scala controls, which argues against a leak.

The refresh lane is now doing three things:
- comparing `a4501839bd` against its exact base, to classify the Scala difference as a leak, base age, or a replay-environment cause;
- checking that d76d8566f8 against its parent reproduces exactly the six work deltas;
- sending me the receipt path and the master SHA before anything is installed.

No reply needed. Nothing of mine is running.
